\title{Composable Certificates and an Infinite Family of Orderly Friedman Numbers}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 15, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An orderly Friedman number is a positive integer whose decimal digits, used exactly once and in their original left-to-right order, can be equipped with arithmetic operations to form an expression equal to the integer itself. We give a self-contained expression model incorporating decimal concatenation and excluding trivial certificates. The identities $-1+2^7=127$ and $7+3^6=736$ certify $127$ and $736$, with the latter showing that orderly Friedman numbers need not be odd. Repeated concatenation of the certificate for $127$ produces
\[
F_0=127,\qquad F_{n+1}=1000F_n+127.
\]
Every $F_n$ is orderly, the exact identity
\[
999F_n=127(1000^{n+1}-1)
\]
holds, and the family is strictly increasing. Hence infinitely many distinct orderly Friedman numbers exist. We also describe validation and generation algorithms, derive the asymptotic scale, and formulate a general certified-block repetition principle.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Friedman numbers turn decimal notation into material for arithmetic self-description. In the orderly variant, digits must remain in precisely the order in which they occur in the original numeral. Thus a candidate identity must satisfy both an arithmetic equation and a syntactic word constraint. For example,
\[
-1+2^7=127
\]
uses the visible digits $1,2,7$ in order, while
\[
7+3^6=736
\]
uses $7,3,6$ in order.

The first identity is also composable. Copies can be joined by decimal concatenation to certify $127127$, $127127127$, and arbitrarily long repetitions. We formulate that observation precisely, prove the resulting recurrence and closed form, and derive infinitude. All conventions needed for the argument are stated below.

\section{Expressions and decimal words}

A \emph{decimal word} is a finite list $d_1,\ldots,d_m$ with $d_i\in\{0,\ldots,9\}$. Define
\[
\operatorname{Dec}(d_1,\ldots,d_m)=\sum_{i=1}^m d_i10^{m-i},
\]
with value $0$ for the empty word.

\begin{lemma}[Decimal concatenation]
If $u$ and $v$ are decimal words, $uv$ denotes their concatenation, and $|v|$ is the length of $v$, then
\[
\operatorname{Dec}(uv)=\operatorname{Dec}(u)10^{|v|}+\operatorname{Dec}(v).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each digit from $u$ acquires $|v|$ additional places to its right, multiplying its contribution by $10^{|v|}$. Digits from $v$ retain their place values. Summing both parts gives the identity.
\end{proof}

Expressions are built from decimal digits using unary negation, addition, multiplication, exponentiation by a written nonnegative integer, and decimal concatenation. The \emph{leaf word} is the sequence of all displayed digits read from left to right, ignoring operation signs and parentheses. Thus the leaf word of $-1+2^7$ is $(1,2,7)$.

Every expression has its usual integer value. If $E$ and $G$ are concatenated with declared right width $k$, write $E\mathbin{\Vert_k}G$ and define
\[
\operatorname{val}(E\mathbin{\Vert_k}G)=10^k\operatorname{val}(E)+\operatorname{val}(G).
\]
Such a node is \emph{well formed} when $k$ is the length of the leaf word of $G$ and both children are well formed. Arithmetic nodes are well formed when their children are. An expression contains \emph{genuine arithmetic} when it uses negation, addition, multiplication, or exponentiation; concatenation alone does not qualify.

\begin{definition}[Orderly Friedman certificate]
Let $N$ be a positive integer with decimal word $w_N$. A well-formed expression $E$ is an orderly Friedman certificate for $N$ if its leaf word is $w_N$, its value is $N$, and it contains genuine arithmetic. A positive integer admitting such a certificate is an \emph{orderly Friedman number}.
\end{definition}

The arithmetic requirement prevents every numeral from certifying itself by mere concatenation. Recording widths handles right blocks with possible leading zeros and makes concatenation semantics unambiguous.

\section{Elementary certificates}

\begin{theorem}
The integer $127$ is an orderly Friedman number.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The expression $-1+2^7$ has leaf word $(1,2,7)$ and value $-1+128=127$. It contains genuine arithmetic and has no concatenation nodes to check.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
The integer $736$ is an orderly Friedman number.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The expression $7+3^6$ has leaf word $(7,3,6)$ and value $7+729=736$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Not every orderly Friedman number is odd.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The orderly Friedman number $736$ is even.
\end{proof}

\section{The repeated-block family}

Define
\[
F_0=127,\qquad F_{n+1}=1000F_n+127.
\]
Let $C=-1+2^7$, and recursively define certificate expressions
\[
R_0=C,\qquad R_{n+1}=R_n\mathbin{\Vert_3}C.
\]
The first numerical terms are $127$, $127127$, $127127127$, and $127127127127$.

\begin{lemma}[Leaf structure]
For every $n\ge0$, the leaf word of $R_n$ consists of $n+1$ consecutive copies of $(1,2,7)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The claim holds for $R_0=C$. The recursive step appends exactly the leaf word $(1,2,7)$ of $C$, so induction proves the result.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Well-formedness]
Every $R_n$ is well formed.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The base expression has no concatenation. At each recursive step both children are well formed and the right child $C$ has exactly three leaves, agreeing with the declared width.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Evaluation]
For every $n\ge0$, $\operatorname{val}(R_n)=F_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Both sides equal $127$ at $n=0$. If equality holds at $n$, then
\[
\operatorname{val}(R_{n+1})=1000\operatorname{val}(R_n)+127=1000F_n+127=F_{n+1}.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Decimal interpretation]
The decimal value of the leaf word of $R_n$ is $F_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The base word $(1,2,7)$ has value $127$. Appending its three digits multiplies the preceding decimal value by $10^3$ and adds $127$, by the decimal-concatenation lemma. This is exactly the recurrence for $F_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinite block family]
For every integer $n\ge0$, the number $F_n$ is an orderly Friedman number.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Use $R_n$ as certificate. The preceding lemmas establish its well-formedness, leaf word, decimal interpretation, and arithmetic value. Each $R_n$ retains the genuine arithmetic operations in $C$. Thus every certificate condition holds.
\end{proof}

This theorem is constructive: from $n$ it gives both the integer and a canonical expression witnessing orderliness.

\section{Closed form, monotonicity, and asymptotics}

\begin{theorem}[Division-free closed form]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
999F_n=127(1000^{n+1}-1).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $n=0$ both sides equal $999\cdot127$. Assuming the result at $n$,
\begin{align*}
999F_{n+1}
&=999(1000F_n+127)\\
&=1000(999F_n)+999\cdot127\\
&=1000\cdot127(1000^{n+1}-1)+999\cdot127\\
&=127(1000^{n+2}-1).
\end{align*}
Induction completes the proof.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Geometric form]
For every $n\ge0$,
\[
F_n=127\sum_{j=0}^{n}1000^j
=\frac{127(1000^{n+1}-1)}{999}.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Iteration of the recurrence gives the sum. Multiplication by $1000-1=999$ telescopes it, yielding the closed identity and exact divisibility.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict growth]
The sequence $(F_n)_{n\ge0}$ is strictly increasing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every term is positive and
\[
F_{n+1}-F_n=999F_n+127>0.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Infinitude]
There are infinitely many distinct orderly Friedman numbers.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Every $F_n$ is orderly and strict growth makes the terms pairwise distinct.
\end{proof}

The exact formula also gives
\[
\frac{F_n}{1000^{n+1}}
=\frac{127}{999}\left(1-1000^{-(n+1)}\right)
\longrightarrow\frac{127}{999}.
\]
Hence $F_n\sim(127/999)1000^{n+1}$. Moreover,
\[
\frac{F_{n+1}}{F_n}=1000+\frac{127}{F_n}\longrightarrow1000.
\]
The growth simply records the addition of three decimal positions at each step.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Certificate validation]
Given an integer $N$ and an expression tree $E$, traverse $E$ in postorder. At each node compute its leaf word, integer value, a well-formedness flag, and a genuine-arithmetic flag. At $A\mathbin{\Vert_k}B$, require $k$ to equal the leaf length of $B$ and compute $10^k\operatorname{val}(A)+\operatorname{val}(B)$. Accept precisely when the final leaf word is the canonical decimal word of $N$, the final value is $N$, and both flags are true.
\end{algorithm}

With fixed-size arithmetic this traversal takes $O(s)$ time for a tree of $s$ nodes and $O(h)$ stack space for height $h$. Arbitrary-precision arithmetic adds costs depending on operand length. Leaf sequences should be accumulated with buffers or ropes to avoid repeated copying.

\begin{algorithm}[Recurrent family generation]
Given $m\ge0$, set $x\leftarrow127$ and output $x$. Repeat $m$ times: set $x\leftarrow1000x+127$ and output $x$. The outputs are $F_0,\ldots,F_m$.
\end{algorithm}

The final value has $3(m+1)$ decimal digits. Multiplication by $1000$ is a decimal shift, so producing only $F_m$ takes work linear in output length. Printing every prefix necessarily writes $\Theta(m^2)$ digits in total. For an independent numerical check, compare the recurrence with exact integer evaluation of $127(1000^{n+1}-1)/999$.

\section{A general block principle}

The construction is not special to three digits except for its seed certificate.

\begin{theorem}[Repetition principle]
Let $B$ be a positive $d$-digit integer with a well-formed orderly certificate containing genuine arithmetic. Define
\[
G_0=B,\qquad G_{n+1}=10^dG_n+B.
\]
Then every $G_n$, obtained by writing $n+1$ copies of the block $B$, is an orderly Friedman number. Furthermore,
\[
(10^d-1)G_n=B(10^{d(n+1)}-1),
\]
and $(G_n)$ is strictly increasing.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Join $n+1$ copies of the certificate by width-$d$ concatenation. Its leaves are the repeated decimal block, and its value obeys the displayed recurrence. Genuine arithmetic remains in every copy. Induction, with $1000$ replaced by $10^d$, proves the closed identity. Finally,
\[
G_{n+1}-G_n=(10^d-1)G_n+B>0.
\]
\end{proof}

This principle identifies a reusable source of infinite families: every suitable certified block is not merely one example but a generator.

\section{Counting consequences}

Let $A(X)$ count orderly Friedman numbers at most $X$. Strict growth makes every family term a distinct contribution. From the closed form, $F_n\le X$ exactly when
\[
1000^{n+1}\le1+\frac{999X}{127}.
\]
Therefore, for $X\ge127$,
\[
A(X)\ge
\left\lfloor
\log_{1000}\left(1+\frac{999X}{127}\right)
\right\rfloor.
\]
This logarithmic lower bound proves infinitude but not positive density. Independent compatible blocks, especially blocks that can be chosen in branching combinations, may lead to stronger counting estimates.

\section{Data cautions and applications}

The certificate $7+3^6=736$ refutes the conjecture that all orderly Friedman numbers are odd. Likewise, a supplied list ending in $14641,155$ is not increasing as written because $155<14641$; the final item may be truncated or transcribed incorrectly, but the literal list cannot be strictly increasing. Finally, the repeated-block family refutes any claim that only finitely many sporadic examples exist, although its exponential spacing does not establish that orderly Friedman numbers are dense.

The construction also illustrates broader ideas. Validation is syntax-directed evaluation coupled to preservation of a source word, as in parsing and symbolic algebra. The seed identity is a composable witness: a local certificate is amplified into global ones by an interface preserving both syntax and value. Finally, recurrence and closed form provide independent exact algorithms for cross-checking numerical output.

Order preservation can aid exhaustive search. For a fixed digit string, dynamic programming may assign attainable values to consecutive intervals and combine adjacent intervals using allowed operations. Because reordering is forbidden, every binary split is an interval split. Bounds on exponents and intermediate values are needed to make such a search finite.

\section{Future work}

A fuller grammar could add factorial, exact roots, division with explicit domain conditions, and arbitrary exponent expressions. A total parser should connect printed notation with the abstract expression semantics and preserve leaf order by construction. Bounded search could certify longer initial segments and investigate whether a terminal $155$ in a supplied list is a complete term or a transcription defect.

On the asymptotic side, several independent block families could improve the lower bound for $A(X)$. If compatible blocks admit independent choices at successive positions, the number of certificates of a fixed length might grow exponentially in the block count, yielding a power-law lower bound in $X$. Natural statistical questions include digit-length distribution, parity, overlap among block families, and dependence on the allowed operation set.

\section{Conclusion}

The three-digit identity $-1+2^7=127$ is a composable orderly certificate. Repeating it gives
\[
127,\ 127127,\ 127127127,\ldots,
\]
with recurrence $F_{n+1}=1000F_n+127$, exact formula
\[
999F_n=127(1000^{n+1}-1),
\]
and strict growth. Thus infinitely many distinct orderly Friedman numbers exist. The even certificate $7+3^6=736$ corrects a tempting parity conjecture. More generally, any certified decimal block generates its own repeated-block family. A numerical recreation thereby becomes a concise theory of composable arithmetic self-description.

\end{document}

